Step 2: Configure Firebase Admin SDK

In your Laravel application, you need to configure the Firebase Admin SDK. First, download the service account key JSON file from your Firebase project's settings. Then, set the environment variable in your .env file:

FIREBASE_CREDENTIALS_PATH=path/to/service-account.json

In your config/app.php, you can set up the Firebase Admin SDK:

'firebase' => [
    'credentials' => env('FIREBASE_CREDENTIALS_PATH'),
]

But instead of using the config, you can directly load the credentials in your code.

Step 3: Generate a Firebase ID Token for Guest User

To authenticate a guest user, you can create a custom token using the Firebase Admin SDK. This token can then be used to authenticate the user on the client side.

In your Laravel controller, you can generate a custom token like this:

use Google\Cloud\Firestore\FirestoreClient;
use Firebase\Auth\TokenGenerator;

public function generateGuestToken()
{
    // Initialize the Firebase Admin SDK
    $serviceAccount = ServiceAccount::fromJsonFile(env('FIREBASE_CREDENTIALS_PATH'));
    $firebase = (new \Firebase\Auth\AuthClient())->withServiceAccount($serviceAccount);

    // Create a custom token for the guest user
    $uid = 'guest_user_' . uniqid(); // Generate a unique ID for the guest
    $customToken = $firebase->createCustomToken($uid);

    return response()->json(['token' => $customToken]);
}

This code creates a unique UID for the guest user and generates a custom token using the Firebase Admin SDK.

